  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Window-relative weighting favors transitions with longer available recurrent context; it does not necessarily favor globally recent user interactions. The report acknowledges window locality but lacks a control separating recency from context length.",
    "The sequential comparisons estimate weighting's effect under BPR-max, but cannot establish whether weighting helps independently of the loss. A weighted-baseline-loss arm would resolve this interaction if affordable.",
    "The actual baseline loss, negative count, sampler and reduction remain unspecified. With one negative and no score regularization, BPR-max equals BPR, so the proposed loss comparison could be redundant.",
    "The component is not sufficient to reproduce an executable trial. Exact integration, matched random streams, validation cadence, checkpoint selection, tie-breaking and measured runtime allocation remain unresolved.",
    "Originality 3 is reasonable. Future potential 7 is speculative, and difficulty 3 describes the loss function more accurately than the complete controlled experiment. No fresh pinned validation results establish improvement."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Determine whether positional weighting targets recent preferences or primarily changes the distribution of recurrent context lengths, and identify an affordable discriminating control.",
      "query": "In GRU sequential recommendation trained on sampled history windows, how does exponentially weighting later next-item losses confound user-history recency with available context length and window inclusion probability? Find methods or ablations separating window-relative position, full-history recency and recurrent context length."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Clarify which comparisons isolate loss effects and which change negative coverage, while avoiding an uninformative one-negative experiment.",
      "query": "Official GRU4Rec BPR-max implementation: how do negative count, shared versus per-transition sampling, duplicate negatives, score activation and score regularization affect comparison with BPR and sampled cross-entropy? Verify the K=1 unregularized equivalence and identify controlled small-negative-pool experiments."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Assess whether the proposed weighting has evidence beyond established ingredients and whether a small additional control is more useful than switching architectures.",
      "query": "Recency-weighted next-item loss for recurrent sequential recommendation: studies comparing unweighted versus weighted BPR and BPR-max with fixed samplers, including loss-by-weighting interactions, effective sample size, popularity bias and degradation of long-term preferences."
    }
  ]
}
